/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Cut the input into narrow strips of width Δτ.
   - t is the output time (s), the instant at which you want y; τ is the dummy integration variable (s) that runs along the input's past and future; x(τ) is the input; h(t−τ) is the impulse response flipped and slid to t; y(t) is the output.
   - Two common cases shorten the limits.
   - Graphical convolution is a five-step routine.
   - The edges of the overlap change at breakpoints: values of t where an edge of h(t−τ) crosses an edge of x(τ).
   - Some syllabi ask for numerical convolution: sample the integrand and add areas.
*/
