/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Write the output as y(t)=∫−∞∞h(τ)x(t−τ)dτ.
   - the condition is necessary and sufficient for a causal LTI system. h(0) may be nonzero: only negative time is ruled out.
   - So e−2tu(t) is causal and u(t+1) is not, because it switches on at t=−1 s.
   - A memoryless system is the special case h(t)=Kδ(t): y(t)=Kx(t) depends only on the present input.
   - h(t) is the impulse response and |h(t)| its absolute value.
   - Sufficient.
   - Necessary.
   - The absolute value matters: without it, positive and negative lobes can cancel and hide an unstable system.
   - Neither property implies the other.
   - In an exam, run the two tests one after the other: first look at h(t) for t<0, then integrate |h(t)| over all time.
*/
