/* book Worked example: Q: Test h₁(t)=e−2tu(t), h₂(t)=e2tu(−t) and h₃(t)=u(t) for causality and BIBO stability. Give the area under |h| in each case.
   1. h₁ is zero for t<0, so it is causal. Area=∫0∞e−2tdt=[−e−2t/2]0∞=0−(−1/2)=1/2. Finite, so stable.
   2. h₂ is nonzero for t<0 (for example h₂(−1)=e−2=0.135), so it is not causal. Area=∫−∞0e2tdt=[e2t/2]−∞0=1/2−0=1/2. Finite, so stable.
   3. h₃ is zero for t<0, so it is causal. Area=∫0∞1 dt, which grows without limit. Not stable: the bounded input u(t) gives the ramp y(t)=t·u(t).
   4. Bound check for h₁: an input with |x|≤5 V gives |y|≤5×1/2=2.5 V.
   Answer: h₁: causal and stable (area 0.5). h₂: noncausal but stable (area 0.5). h₃: causal but unstable (area ∞). */
